1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,467
Hi Ilyasa.

2
00:00:01,467 --> 00:00:04,833
This video walks you through your PSM2 project.

3
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:06,600
We will cover four things.

4
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:08,100
What the system does.

5
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:09,500
How it is wired.

6
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:12,533
Which commands to run and how to read the console.

7
00:00:12,533 --> 00:00:14,967
And how to read the machine learning results.

8
00:00:15,767 --> 00:00:20,033
The PZEM sensor measures how much power one appliance is using.

9
00:00:20,033 --> 00:00:24,333
The ESP32 sends that reading over WiFi to Blynk.

10
00:00:24,333 --> 00:00:31,667
A Python script on the laptop reads it from Blynk, and an LSTM model predicts the power for the next thirty minutes.

11
00:00:31,667 --> 00:00:37,000
The script compares the prediction with a limit, makes a decision, and writes it back to Blynk.

12
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,500
The ESP32 picks up the decision and acts, using the relay, the buzzer and the LCD.

13
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:47,133
The laptop never talks to the ESP32 directly.

14
00:00:47,133 --> 00:00:48,967
Everything goes through Blynk.

15
00:00:49,767 --> 00:00:51,867
There are four possible decisions.

16
00:00:51,867 --> 00:00:53,600
OK means no action.

17
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:55,667
NOTIFY is a warning only.

18
00:00:55,667 --> 00:00:58,600
It is used for the laptop, which is never cut.

19
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:02,133
SHIFT is advice to use the appliance at a cheaper time.

20
00:01:02,133 --> 00:01:05,100
It is used for the fan, and the relay stays on.

21
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:08,067
CUTOFF is for the water heater and the test lamp.

22
00:01:08,067 --> 00:01:11,900
The buzzer warns for thirty seconds, then the relay cuts the power.

23
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:21,800
The limit gets tighter when this month's usage is close to the next TNB price bracket, or during the Time of Use peak, from 2 pm to 10 pm on weekdays.

24
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:24,300
Here is the full circuit.

25
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:32,400
On the left is the low voltage side: the ESP32, the LCD, the DHT22 sensor and the buzzer.

26
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:39,600
On the right is the mains side: the PZEM with its current coil, the relay, and the socket where the appliance plugs in.

27
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:44,300
The lamp in this picture stands for whatever appliance is plugged into the socket.

28
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:46,467
These are the pins.

29
00:01:46,467 --> 00:01:49,867
GPIO 26 goes to the relay input.

30
00:01:49,867 --> 00:01:52,867
GPIO 27 goes to the buzzer.

31
00:01:52,867 --> 00:02:01,200
The PZEM uses serial: its TX goes to GPIO 16, and its RX goes to GPIO 17.

32
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:09,467
The LCD uses I2C on GPIO 21 for SDA and GPIO 22 for SCL.

33
00:02:09,467 --> 00:02:13,700
The DHT22 data pin goes to GPIO 4.

34
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:19,067
The relay, the PZEM and the LCD take power from VIN, which is 5 volts.

35
00:02:19,067 --> 00:02:21,300
And every ground is joined together.

36
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:25,233
On the mains side, only the live wire is switched.

37
00:02:25,233 --> 00:02:31,833
From the plug, live goes through the 10 amp fuse and through the CT coil, into the relay COM terminal.

38
00:02:31,833 --> 00:02:35,067
The relay NO terminal goes to the socket live.

39
00:02:35,067 --> 00:02:37,233
Neutral and earth go straight through.

40
00:02:37,233 --> 00:02:43,700
The PZEM voltage terminals tap live and neutral before the relay, so voltage is always measured.

41
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:45,900
Safety first.

42
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:49,067
Always unplug the box before you open or touch it.

43
00:02:49,067 --> 00:02:53,133
Test with the lamp first, then the fan, and only then the water heater.

44
00:02:53,133 --> 00:03:02,467
One thing to check before your demo: the relay board is a 5 volt active low board, and with a 3.3 volt signal it can stay on all the time.

45
00:03:02,467 --> 00:03:08,200
Fix it with the JD VCC jumper, or set RELAY OPEN DRAIN to true in the sketch.

46
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:12,867
Then run the pin test sketch in mode 2, and make sure the relay clicks on and off.

47
00:03:13,667 --> 00:03:16,233
In Blynk, V0 is the live power.

48
00:03:16,233 --> 00:03:23,200
V10 is the decision, from 0 for OK up to 3 for CUTOFF, and V11 is the reason text.

49
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:25,367
V12 shows the relay state.

50
00:03:25,367 --> 00:03:31,633
V13 lets you force the relay on or off by hand, and V14 lets you mute the buzzer.

51
00:03:31,633 --> 00:03:38,367
V15 turns the wattage mapping on or off, and V16 to V20 show the tariff and the cost.

52
00:03:38,367 --> 00:03:42,733
The switches only work on the Devices page, not on the template page.

53
00:03:42,733 --> 00:03:45,333
Your auth token is in Device Info.

54
00:03:45,333 --> 00:03:48,267
Keep it private, and never show it in a recording.

55
00:03:49,067 --> 00:03:50,667
Setup is done once.

56
00:03:50,667 --> 00:03:55,833
Open a Command Prompt in the scripts folder and install the Python packages with pip.

57
00:03:55,833 --> 00:03:59,967
Then open live control loop dot py and paste your Blynk token.

58
00:03:59,967 --> 00:04:10,467
In the Arduino IDE, open the main sketch, choose ESP32 Dev Module, fill in the template ID, the token and your WiFi, and upload.

59
00:04:10,467 --> 00:04:18,767
For quick testing, set LIVE SEND MS to five thousand, so the ESP32 sends a reading every five seconds.

60
00:04:19,567 --> 00:04:21,600
To run it, use two terminals.

61
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:27,367
In the first one, start the live loop and tell it which appliance is plugged in, for example the lamp.

62
00:04:27,367 --> 00:04:33,133
In the second, start the dashboard with streamlit, or just double click run dashboard dot bat.

63
00:04:33,133 --> 00:04:35,033
It opens in your browser.

64
00:04:35,033 --> 00:04:39,533
If you have no hardware with you, demo decision now gives instant results.

65
00:04:39,533 --> 00:04:44,167
Add dash dash push to send the result to Blynk, and the LCD will show it.

66
00:04:44,967 --> 00:04:47,567
When the live loop starts, it prints a header.

67
00:04:47,567 --> 00:04:50,400
The first line says if wattage mapping is on.

68
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:54,967
The small lamp is scaled up to the size of appliance the model was trained on.

69
00:04:54,967 --> 00:04:58,333
The second line shows the tariff and the price right now.

70
00:04:58,333 --> 00:05:01,800
Fast test mode means it reads every thirty seconds.

71
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:06,067
That tests the whole chain, but it is not a true thirty minute forecast.

72
00:05:06,067 --> 00:05:12,867
The last line shows the appliance and this month's kilowatt hours, which are used for the TNB bracket check.

73
00:05:13,667 --> 00:05:18,033
The first four readings only fill the window, so there is no decision yet.

74
00:05:18,033 --> 00:05:21,133
From the fourth reading, each line shows three numbers.

75
00:05:21,133 --> 00:05:22,933
Now is the live reading.

76
00:05:22,933 --> 00:05:26,100
Forecast is what the LSTM expects next.

77
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:27,933
Limit is the threshold.

78
00:05:27,933 --> 00:05:31,533
If the forecast is above the limit, the decision changes.

79
00:05:31,533 --> 00:05:37,500
The decision is sent to Blynk only when it changes, so the buzzer does not beep every thirty seconds.

80
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:44,200
If you see could not read V0, check that the ESP32 is on, the WiFi, and the token.

81
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:50,700
If V0 has not changed for six readings, set LIVE SEND MS to five thousand in the sketch.

82
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:53,500
The LCD has two rows.

83
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:55,533
The top row shows the decision.

84
00:05:55,533 --> 00:05:58,400
The star at the end means Blynk is connected.

85
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:01,000
An exclamation mark means it is not.

86
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,667
The second row shows power, voltage and temperature, or the reason text when there is a warning.

87
00:06:06,667 --> 00:06:11,100
CUTOFF in 25 seconds is the countdown before the relay cuts.

88
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:20,467
FAILSAFE means the cloud was lost for more than five minutes, or Python sent nothing for 45 minutes, so the relay is switched back on to be safe.

89
00:06:21,267 --> 00:06:22,967
Now the machine learning.

90
00:06:22,967 --> 00:06:30,933
The models were trained on the iAWE dataset, which is real power data from homes in Delhi, in fifteen minute steps.

91
00:06:30,933 --> 00:06:37,833
The model looks at the last four readings, which is one hour, and predicts the next two steps, which is thirty minutes.

92
00:06:37,833 --> 00:06:42,200
It is two LSTM layers of 64 units with dropout.

93
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:44,300
There is one model per appliance.

94
00:06:44,300 --> 00:06:53,933
Be honest about this part: the water heater model was trained on an air conditioner channel, so it is a proxy, and the fan model was trained on a TV channel.

95
00:06:53,933 --> 00:06:57,533
ARIMA is used as the baseline to compare against.

96
00:06:58,333 --> 00:07:01,700
This graph is the easiest way to see if the model works.

97
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:07,533
Blue is the real power, and orange is the prediction, on test data the model never saw.

98
00:07:07,533 --> 00:07:12,267
When the heater is steadily on or off, the orange line sits right on the blue line.

99
00:07:12,267 --> 00:07:16,400
When the heater suddenly switches, the prediction is about one step late.

100
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:21,600
That is normal for this kind of model, because it can only learn from what it has already seen.

101
00:07:22,400 --> 00:07:24,400
The loss curve shows the training.

102
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:31,400
Blue is the error on training data, and orange is the error on validation data the model did not train on.

103
00:07:31,400 --> 00:07:33,600
Both drop quickly and stay low.

104
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:38,167
Because the orange line does not climb back up, the model is not overfitting.

105
00:07:38,967 --> 00:07:40,433
Here are the numbers.

106
00:07:40,433 --> 00:07:45,800
RMSE and MAE are the average error in watts, so smaller is better.

107
00:07:45,800 --> 00:07:48,433
Compare them with the size of the appliance.

108
00:07:48,433 --> 00:07:55,300
An error of four hundred watts sounds big, but the heater swings between zero and almost two thousand watts.

109
00:07:55,300 --> 00:08:00,967
R squared is a score where one is perfect, and zero means no better than guessing the average.

110
00:08:00,967 --> 00:08:05,300
The water heater model is the strongest, at 0.70.

111
00:08:05,300 --> 00:08:07,933
The laptop is 0.50.

112
00:08:07,933 --> 00:08:15,667
The fan is the weakest at 0.30, because its power hardly changes, so there is very little pattern to learn.

113
00:08:16,467 --> 00:08:19,767
This chart compares the LSTM with ARIMA.

114
00:08:19,767 --> 00:08:21,233
Be careful with it.

115
00:08:21,233 --> 00:08:27,367
The two models were tested on different samples, so the RMSE bars are not a fair head to head.

116
00:08:27,367 --> 00:08:34,967
Using R squared, the LSTM is better for the water heater, 0.64 against 0.43.

117
00:08:34,967 --> 00:08:37,233
ARIMA is better for the laptop.

118
00:08:37,233 --> 00:08:40,000
Say this openly in your report and viva.

119
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,467
It is a real result, not a mistake.

120
00:08:43,267 --> 00:08:45,700
This is the best thing to show in your viva.

121
00:08:45,700 --> 00:08:49,867
It is the same water heater, using the same fifteen hundred watts.

122
00:08:49,867 --> 00:08:55,967
In the middle of the month, the limit is sixteen hundred and twenty watts, so the system only gives a warning.

123
00:08:55,967 --> 00:09:05,667
When the month's usage is just five kilowatt hours from the two hundred bracket, the limit drops to twelve hundred and fifty five watts, and the same heater is cut off.

124
00:09:05,667 --> 00:09:08,767
That is the predictive, tariff aware part working.

125
00:09:09,567 --> 00:09:11,333
Finally, the dashboard.

126
00:09:11,333 --> 00:09:15,033
The control panel is where you start a demo or the live loop.

127
00:09:15,033 --> 00:09:22,000
The Today tab shows power and price for today, with the real reading in blue and the LSTM in orange.

128
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:24,833
Live decisions is the main demo page.

129
00:09:24,833 --> 00:09:29,867
Model shows the accuracy, and Decision logic lets you try your own numbers.

130
00:09:29,867 --> 00:09:33,567
When you finish, press stop live loop before you close the dashboard.

131
00:09:34,367 --> 00:09:37,667
To finish, a few things to say honestly to your panel.

132
00:09:37,667 --> 00:09:40,667
The heater model is an air conditioner proxy.

133
00:09:40,667 --> 00:09:44,500
The live demo shows the mechanism working from end to end.

134
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:46,300
It is not a measured saving.

135
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:50,533
Wattage mapping fixes the size of the reading, not the usage pattern.

136
00:09:50,533 --> 00:09:53,700
And demo scenarios are not live sensor data.

137
00:09:53,700 --> 00:09:55,500
That is the whole project.

138
00:09:55,500 --> 00:09:57,033
Good luck with your viva.
